<!-- Aegis security manifest. Harmattan's mandatory access control: a binary
     that touches a protected resource without a grant fails silently at
     runtime, with no dialog and no log entry. -->

<!-- XML comments are allowed here, but a double hyphen inside one is illegal
     XML and aegis reports it as "Manifest cannot have <! ..> elements", which
     reads like a ban on comments. package.sh refuses to build unless this file
     parses, because aegis only checks it at install time on the device. -->

<!-- NOT VERIFIABLE on the development phone. `accli -I` there reports
     "Current mode: open", and open mode grants no credentials and enforces
     nothing, so the app behaves identically with this file, without it, or
     with it wrong. The first install on a closed device is the only test. -->

<!-- What is requested:

     UID::user, GID::users: run as the normal user, which is all WebMee needs
     for its own IPC. The shell talks to the engine over QProcess pipes, and
     WebKit talks to its own web and network processes over socketpairs it
     creates itself; those are same-uid children of the app, which aegis does
     not gate.

     What is deliberately NOT requested:

     The applauncherd-launcher booster grant. That entry propagates CAPABILITY
     credentials to the launcher so a boosted app does not lose them. WebMee
     asks for no capabilities, so it had nothing to carry, and aegis answered
     on install with "request 'UID::user' for 'applauncherd-launcher::...'
     ignored, matched by deny rule": identity tokens cannot be propagated that
     way. Add the entry when there is a real capability to carry, not before.

     A signond credential, for the credential store behind the HTTP
     authentication prompt (shell/passwordstore.cpp). It is the one system
     service this app calls, over the session bus. The token definitions under
     /usr/share/aegis-manifest-dev/api are not installed on the build host or
     the phone, so `aegis-manifest` cannot generate one, and a guessed token
     name fails the install outright. If saved passwords do not work on a
     closed device, this is the first thing to look at.

     Regenerate with `aegis-manifest` against the built binaries rather than
     extending this by hand, and add only what it reports missing. -->

<aegis>
	<request policy="add">
		<credential name="UID::user" />
		<credential name="GID::users" />
		<for path="/opt/n9browser/bin/WebMee" />
	</request>

	<request policy="add">
		<credential name="UID::user" />
		<credential name="GID::users" />
		<for path="/opt/n9browser/bin/webmee-engine" />
	</request>
</aegis>
